  "label": "TMUA 2017 · Paper 1 · Question 6",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2017 · Paper 1 · Question 6 — Find the positive y-intercept of a tangent from (20,0) to a radius-12 circle.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "B",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Draw the radius to the tangent",
      "body": "<p>Call the origin O, the given point P, and the contact point T. Join O to T.</p>",
      "recap": "The radius at a tangent contact is perpendicular to the tangent.",
      "pitfall": "The circle equation gives the squared radius, not the radius.",
      "pause": "Find OP and OT, then use the right triangle OPT to find PT.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l03",
          "reminder": "The radius at a tangent contact is perpendicular to the tangent."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Find the second right triangle",
      "body": "<p>Call the positive y-axis intercept B. Compare triangles OPT and POB: both are right-angled and share the angle at P.</p>",
      "recap": "Triangles with the same angles have proportional corresponding sides.",
      "pitfall": "Match sides using their opposite angles before writing a ratio.",
      "pause": "Use the two triangles to find OB.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-similar-triangles",
          "reminder": "Triangles with the same angles have proportional corresponding sides."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Let O be the origin, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and T the contact point. The radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math> is perpendicular to the tangent, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>12</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the right triangles OPT and POB share their angle at P. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>O</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></math>. The condition that the tangent crosses the positive y-axis selects this positive value.</p><p><strong>Answer B: 15.</strong></p>",
